- How would you adapt if at-most-k subarrays were allowed instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests an advanced DP on trees with knapsack-style merging across children, handling disjoint rooted subtrees. Requires precise base cases, reconstruction logic, and clarity on disjointness constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with limited subtrees (knapsack on trees)
- Rooted-subtree definition to enforce disjointness
- Merging child subproblems via convolution-like transitions
- Precomputing maxSub[u] for O(1) subtree value

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-structure illustrating DP arrays
function dfs(u, parent){
  maxSub[u] = max(val[u], ...subtreeVals);
  dp[u] = Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [ -INF, -INF ]);
  dp[u][0][0] = 0;
  for (let v of children[u]){
    dfs(v, u);
    // merge: newDp[t][0] = max over s of dp[u][t-s][0] + best[v][s]
  }
